<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Geats</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Götar, Gautar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Geats were a North Germanic people of what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vastergotland">Västergötland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostergotland">Östergötland</a> around lakes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-vanern">Vänern</a> and Vättern. They appear in Ptolemy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> and as the hero's people in the Old English poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beowulf">Beowulf</a>. Around 1000 they came under the kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svear">Svear</a>, and their union with them formed the medieval Kingdom of Sweden.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geats.jpg" alt="Geats" width="320" height="592" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The map is based on https://web.archive.org/web/20050415210943/http://www.umu.se/archaeology/publikation/ae/hogom.htm... · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nordic_Settlements.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
